          "text": "{\n  \"target_identification\": {\n    \"speaker\": \"Unnamed first-person narrator\",\n    \"addressee\": \"Maria\",\n    \"anchor_quote\": \"Saavist som jeg altid hellere har sagt Dig for lidt\\nend for meget, saavist elsker jeg Dig. Jeg elsker Dig i Lyst og i\\nNød, Hverdage og Festdage. Du er den, jeg vil dø med, men først vil\\njeg leve meget, meget længe med Dig. Javel har jeg længtes efter\\nmin yndige Elskerindes Kærtegn. Men mest har jeg længtes efter alt\\ndet andet, som ogsaa er Dig: dit trofaste Hjærte, din rene Tanke,\\ndin milde Tale, dit lyse Smil, som er min Sol i Tvivlen, dit gode\\nHaandtryk, som er min Styrke paa Vejen.\"\n  },\n  \"target_translation\": \"I love you.\",\n  \"dimensions\": {\n    \"T\": {\n      \"score\": 4,\n      \"evidence_status\": \"assessable\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.99,\n      \"reason\": \"The focal expression directly avows the speaker's present love for Maria. It answers her request for assurance that his apparent change is genuine and not something he will regret tomorrow.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"block_id\": \"target\",\n          \"quotation\": \"elsker jeg Dig\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"block_id\": \"scene\",\n          \"quotation\": \"Jeg kan jo ikke tvivle om Marias Kærlighed, jeg tvivler ikke længer\\nom min egen.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"P\": {\n      \"score\": 3,\n      \"evidence_status\": \"assessable\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.93,\n      \"reason\": \"In this occurrence, the avowal is framed as a solemn assurance and immediately expanded into an affirmation of enduring companionship through pleasure and hardship and of a shared life. Thus the focal expression helps affirm a continuing relational undertaking, rather than merely standing next to an unrelated promise. The commitment is substantial but its practical terms are not fully specified.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"block_id\": \"scene\",\n          \"quotation\": \"Saavist som jeg altid hellere har sagt Dig for lidt\\nend for meget, saavist elsker jeg Dig. Jeg elsker Dig i Lyst og i\\nNød, Hverdage og Festdage. Du er den, jeg vil dø med, men først vil\\njeg leve meget, meget længe med Dig.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"block_id\": \"scene\",\n          \"quotation\": \"For det er jo sandt, ikke noget, Du imorgen fortryder, ikke\\nnoget, Du nu bilder Dig ind, fordi Du har længtes en Smule efter din\\nElskerinde?\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"E\": {\n      \"score\": 0,\n      \"evidence_status\": \"assessable\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.91,\n      \"reason\": \"Although the reunion is intensely emotional, the focal words are presented as a deliberate, syntactically developed answer to Maria's question, not as a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"block_id\": \"scene\",\n          \"quotation\": \"Jeg svarer: »Saavist som jeg altid hellere har sagt Dig for lidt\\nend for meget, saavist elsker jeg Dig.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"O\": {\n      \"score\": 0,\n      \"evidence_status\": \"assessable\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.9,\n      \"reason\": \"The expression's reassurance and confirmation functions are adequately captured by its avowal of love and contextual affirmation of an enduring bond. No distinct residual core force is needed.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"block_id\": \"scene\",\n          \"quotation\": \"Men da jeg siger Maria dette, græder hun, fordi hun forstaar, jeg\\ntaler sandt, og fordi hun er lykkelig.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  },\n  \"original_speech_situation\": \"At an emotionally intense reunion, Maria asks whether the narrator's apparent love is genuine, lasting, and free from likely regret. He answers her directly with the focal avowal, solemnly reinforced and followed by statements of love in both joy and hardship and a desire to live a long life with her.\",\n  \"affection_evidence\": \"present\",\n  \"undertaking_and_uptake\": \"The focal avowal, through its oath-like framing and its continuation in the same answer, affirms that the speaker is committed to loving and sharing life with Maria through changing circumstances. The earlier agreement about dying together is a separate prior exchange, although the present turn invokes that shared prospect. Maria's tears and happiness represent positive uptake: she understands him as speaking truthfully and accepts the assurance. The evidence does not specify formal or legal terms or establish eventual fulfilment.\",\n  \"recognised_work\": false,\n  \"recognition_note\": \"I do not recognize the work from prior familiarity.\",\n  \"limitations\": \"The passage supplies strong represented evidence of avowal, commitment, affection, and immediate uptake, but literary narration cannot independently verify sincerity or later fulfilment. No conclusions are drawn from context outside the supplied blocks.\"\n}"
